API (Application Programming Interface)
	
1. What is an API?
	An API is an agreed interface through which two pieces of software communicate.
	It defines:
		What you can request
		What data you need to send
		What you get back
		What errors can occur
		How authentication works
		What you're allowed to access
		Limits such as rate limits and request size

2. API as a Boundary
	it creates a boundary between client and internal systems
	This allows the backend to change internally without breaking clients.
	Benefits
		Hides implementation details
		Provides controlled access
		Makes systems easier to change
		Allows multiple clients to reuse the same functionality
		Creates clear ownership between services
		Makes logging, monitoring and tracing easier
		
3. API Contract
	The API contract is the agreement between client and server.
	it should define - What can i do? what do i send? what do i receive? what errors can happend? and all that.
	e.g - GET /v1/orders/ord_123 
	the contract defines - 
		Method       → GET
		Path         → /v1/orders/ord_123
		Headers      → Authorization, Accept
		Response     → JSON
		Errors       → 401, 404, 500...
		
4. Parts of an HTTP API Request
	 it contains
	 	| Part        | Purpose              | Example                          |
		| ----------- | -------------------- | -------------------------------- |
		| **Method**  | Action               | `GET`, `POST`, `PATCH`, `DELETE` |
		| **Path**    | Resource/action      | `/users/123`                     |
		| **Query**   | Filtering/pagination | `?limit=50`                      |
		| **Headers** | Metadata/auth        | `Authorization`                  |
		| **Body**    | Input data           | JSON                             |
		
	e.g - 
		POST /users?sendEmail=true

		Authorization: Bearer <token>
		Content-Type: application/json

		{
		  "name": "John",
		  "email": "john@example.com"
		}
5. HTTP API Response
	A response contains Status code, Headers, Body
	e.g - 
		HTTP/1.1 201 Created

		{
		  "id": 123,
		  "name": "John"
		}
	important status codes - 
		200 → Success
		201 → Resource created
		400 → Bad request
		401 → Not authenticated
		403 → Not authorized
		404 → Not found
		429 → Rate limited
		500 → Server error
		
6. Types of APIs
	Public API
		Used by external developers/customers.
		needs - Documentation, Authentication , Authorization, rate limiting, stable contracts, monitoring, versioning.
	Partner API
		Used by selected external organizations.
		needs - Tenant/partner access portal, Auditing, Data-sharing rules, Contract management.
	Internal API
		Used between services inside an organization.
		e.g - checkout -> payment to inventory to fraud to notification.
	Library API
		Used inside the same application.
		The caller uses the function without knowing its internal implementation.

7. Common API Styles
	| Style                   | Common use                                |
	| ----------------------- | ----------------------------------------- |
	| **REST**                | Public APIs, web/mobile backends          |
	| **GraphQL**             | Flexible data queries                     |
	| **gRPC**                | Internal service-to-service communication |
	| **WebSocket**           | Real-time two-way communication           |
	| **SSE**                 | Server → client streaming                 |
	| **Webhooks**            | Event callbacks                           |
	| **Message-driven APIs** | Background workflows                      |
	| **SOAP**                | Legacy enterprise integrations            |
	
8. Authentication vs Authorization
	Authentication -
		who are you?
		e.g - API key, OAuth access token, session cookie
	Authorization - 
		Are you allowed to do this?
		e.g - user is Authenticated but is the user allowed to call request: GET /users/456/invoices

9. Rate Limits & Quotas
	Rate limiting protects the API from excessive traffic.
	e.g - 100 requests/minute/user, if exceeded = 429 Too Many Requests
	Quota is usually a broader usage allowance.
	e.g - 10,000 requests/month for free user and 1,00,000 for paid plan
	
10. API Reliability
	Production APIs need to handle failures properly.
	Timeout - 
		Every API call should have a timeout. Without timeouts, connections and threads can pile up.
	Retry - 
		Retries can recover from temporary failures. But GET is usually safe to retry , POST /payment -> Dangerousto blindly retry. Because the first request may have succeeded even though the client never received the response.
		
11. Idempotency
	repeating the same operation should have the same intended effect as performing it once.
	e.g - POST /payments Idempotency-Key: abc123. If client retries - Request 1 → abc123 → Payment created Request 2 → abc123 → Already processed. The server doesn't create a second payment.
	Useful for payments, order creation, job submission.
	
12. API Observability
	Production APIs should record enough information to debug failures.
	useful information - 
		Request ID / Trace ID
		Caller / Tenant
		HTTP method
		Route
		Status code
		Latency
		Request size
		Response size
		Rate-limit information
		Downstream service latency
		
13. API Versioning
	APIs evolve.
	Instead of suddenly breaking existing clients: /v1/users, /v2/users.
	you can introduce a new version while continuing to support the old one.
	
A good API isn't merely an endpoint that returns JSON. It's a stable, predictable contract that safely exposes a capability while allowing the system behind it to evolve.

