REST vs GraphQL

1. REST (Representational State Transfer) - 
	REST is an architectural style, not a protocol. It uses HTTP and organizes APIs around resources.
	The server controls the response structure.
	advantages - Simple and intuitive, stateless, Http caching works naturally, easy to scale with load balancers.
	problems - over fetching (get more data than you need), under fetching (need multiple requests), Rigid response
	
2. GraphQL -
	It is a query language and API runtime where the client specifies exactly what data it wants.
	Usually there is a single endpoint, the client can request related data together, The server returns exactly those requested fields.
	advantages - avoids over fetching, avoids under fetching, one request can retrieve related resources, Easy for different clients to request different data.
	
3. Three Core GraphQL Operations - 
	Query -
		used to read data, compared to GET in REST.
	Mutation - 
		Used to modify data, create, update, delete.
	Subscription - 
		Used for real time updates, server pushes data to client.
		used for chat, Notifications, Live feeds, Stock updates.
		
4. REST vs GraphQL
	|                | REST                              | GraphQL                             |
	| -------------- | --------------------------------- | ----------------------------------- |
	| API structure  | Multiple endpoints                | Usually one endpoint                |
	| Data control   | Server decides                    | Client decides                      |
	| Over-fetching  | Common                            | Reduced                             |
	| Under-fetching | Common                            | Reduced                             |
	| Schema         | Less strict                       | Strongly typed                      |
	| Caching        | Easy with HTTP                    | More complicated                    |
	| Versioning     | Often needed for breaking changes | Often less necessary                |
	| Real-time      | Usually WebSockets/SSE            | Subscriptions                       |
	| Complexity     | Lower                             | Higher                              |
	| Security       | Relatively straightforward        | Query complexity must be controlled |

5. GraphQL's Biggest Problem: Query Complexity 
	The flexibility of GraphQL can become a problem.
	client can request - 
		User
		 └── Posts
		      └── Comments
			   └── Authors
				└── Posts
				     └── Comments
				          ...
	A badly designed/deeply nested query can generate enormous database work.
	Therefore it's systems often needs - Query depth limits, Query cost analysis, Rate limiting, Pagination, Timeouts, Caching.
	
6. GraphQL Caching Is Harder
	REST works nicely with standard HTTP caching: GET /users/123 -> Cache
	GraphQL commonly sends queries through a shared endpoint: POST /graphql, But different queries can request completely different data: A -> name + email, B -> name + posts, C -> post + comments.
	Therefore, traditional URL-based HTTP caching becomes more difficult.
	
7. When to Use REST
	when API is relatively simple, resources map naturally to endpoints, HTTP caching is important.

8. When to use GraphQL
	Different clients need different fields, data is deeply nested, you want to reduce multiple client requests.

9. You Can Use Both 
	A system can use both.
	A common approach is 
		GraphQL -> flexible client facing applications.
		REST -> ADMIN APIs, third-party integrations, simple/internal APIs.
		
