WebHooks

1. What is a Webhook?
	Webhook = an HTTP request sent by one system to another when an event occurs.
	It is a push-based communication pattern.
		Without Webhook: Polling
		Your App ── "Anything changed?" ──> Provider
		Your App ── "Anything changed?" ──> Provider
		Your App ── "Anything changed?" ──> Provider


		With Webhook:
		Provider ── Event happened! ──> Your App
	Instead of continuously asking a provider for updates, the provider calls your endpoint when something happens.
	e.g - stripe -> payment succeded, Github -> pull request opened
	
2. How Webhooks Work
	basic flow = Register endpoint -> Event occurs -> Provider sends POST -> Verify webhook -> save/queue event -> Return 2xx -> Process asynchronously.
	The important idea is acknowledge quickly and do heavy work asynchronously.
	
3. Webhook Request
	Headers -
		| Header       | Purpose                      |
		| ------------ | ---------------------------- |
		| Content-Type | Usually `application/json`   |
		| Event type   | What happened                |
		| Delivery ID  | Identifies delivery/event    |
		| Signature    | Verifies sender              |
		| Timestamp    | Helps prevent replay attacks |
		| User-Agent   | Identifies provider          |
	Body - 
		Contains - Event envelope + Resource data
		e.g - {
			  "event": "payment_succeeded",
			  "id": "evt_123",
			  "data": {
			    "payment_id": "pay_456",
			    "amount": 1000
			  }
			}
			
4. Webhook Receiver
	A production receiver should: Request -> Verify signature -> Validate event -> Check duplicate -> Persist/enqueue -> Return 2xx -> Process Asynchronously
	Important - dont do everything in one request , make queues so that you can response fast so that the provider shouldn't wait and retry.
	
5. At-Least-Once Delivery
	This is one of the most important webhook concepts.
	Meaning = Event -> Provider sends -> Your server processes it -> Response gets lost -> Provider things delivery failed -> Provider sends again. So your application must handle duplicates.
	Solution: Idempotency / Deduplication , Store a unique event or delivery ID. Use a unique database constraint where possible.
	At least once deliver + Idempotency = safe webhook processing.
	
6. Don't Trust Event Ordering
	Webhooks can arrive out of order. Retries and network delays can cause this.
	Solutions
		Don't blindly depend on event order.
		Fetch the latest resource state from the provider when necessary.
		Use resource versions/timestamps.
		Reject invalid state transitions.
		Periodically reconcile your state with the provider.
		
7. Webhook Security
	1. Signature Verification
		provider: HMAC(request_body,shared_secret)
		your server calculates the same signature. Provider signature = your calculated signature -> valid.
	2. Replay Protection
		An attacker could capture a valid webhook and send it again.
		Protection - Timestamp validation, Event/deliver ID deduplication, Store processed events, Idempotent operations
	3. IP Allow Lists
		IP allow-listing should not replace signature verification.
	4. Don't Leak Secrets
		Never log secrets, Tokens, Authorization headers.
		
